import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,j as s}from"./chunks/framework.DsyGq6Zh.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"What I Ask Students to Show When AI Helps Write the Code","description":"Three lessons from teaching web development and AI at NJIT, starting with a shirt, a checkout page, and one press of the refresh button.","frontmatter":{"title":"What I Ask Students to Show When AI Helps Write the Code","description":"Three lessons from teaching web development and AI at NJIT, starting with a shirt, a checkout page, and one press of the refresh button."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md","filePath":"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md"}'),n={name:"teaching/when-ai-helps-write-the-code.md"};function r(h,e,i,d,c,u){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="what-i-ask-students-to-show-when-ai-helps-write-the-code" tabindex="-1">What I Ask Students to Show When AI Helps Write the Code <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-i-ask-students-to-show-when-ai-helps-write-the-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What I Ask Students to Show When AI Helps Write the Code&quot;">​</a></h1><p><em>Keith Williams · November 25, 2026</em></p><p>Buy a shirt. Refresh the page. Can you still find your order?</p><p>That’s a question another teacher could borrow from my introductory web development course at NJIT. The checkout is simulated. The design problem is real. A student can show me a beautiful confirmation screen, and I’ll still have a question about what happened to the order.</p><p>After 23 years of teaching, AI has me taking a closer look at my own assignments. I want students to use the tools they’ll encounter at work. I also want to hear the decisions they made while using them. This fall, revising projects across three courses brought a few lessons into focus for me.</p><h2 id="give-the-demo-a-second-step" tabindex="-1">Give the demo a second step <a class="header-anchor" href="#give-the-demo-a-second-step" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Give the demo a second step&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In my introductory course, students design a storefront around a plain T-shirt. One way to review it is to hand the keyboard to a classmate. Ask them to buy a shirt and find the order after a refresh. Let them try it without coaching. If they get stuck, where did they expect to click? That observation gives the designer a specific change to make and the classmate something to try again.</p><p>In my web applications course, the same idea works one level deeper. A team builds for a simulated customer. Suppose someone submits a service request and sees “Request received.” Restart the app and switch to the coordinator’s view. Can the person responsible find that request and act on it? Now the team has to account for both people in the transaction. Before anyone clicks, I can ask the student running the demo what they expect the coordinator to see and why.</p><h2 id="give-students-a-reason-to-say-we-don-t-know" tabindex="-1">Give students a reason to say “we don’t know” <a class="header-anchor" href="#give-students-a-reason-to-say-we-don-t-know" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Give students a reason to say “we don’t know”&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In my content management course, students build an AI tool that answers questions using a collection of documents. A useful review takes two questions. Pick one the documents can answer and ask the student to show the passage that supports it. Then ask for a detail the documents leave out.</p><p>If the tool supplies that missing detail anyway, we have something worth investigating. Which passage did the search return? What did the model add? A student who can locate the mistake and explain what the system should say has given me a much better look at their judgment. I want the assignment to reward that moment, even when the demo takes an unexpected turn.</p><h2 id="cut-the-paperwork-that-repeats-the-lesson" tabindex="-1">Cut the paperwork that repeats the lesson <a class="header-anchor" href="#cut-the-paperwork-that-repeats-the-lesson" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cut the paperwork that repeats the lesson&quot;">​</a></h2><p>I had to apply the same scrutiny to my side of the course. It’s easy to ask for a project plan, a progress update, and a portfolio reflection, then discover I’m reading the same decision three times. Every extra write-up takes time students could spend putting the fix back in someone’s hands.</p><p>If a classmate reports a confusing checkout in a GitHub issue, the student can link the change there and ask the classmate to retry it. In the portfolio, one sentence can point back to that exchange and name what the student learned. I’d much rather spend our next minute together asking, “What would make you change your mind about that fix?”</p><p>That’s part of how I’m preparing students for forward-deployed engineering, where engineers work directly with the people who need a problem solved. I want my students to feel comfortable handing a customer the keyboard, watching where they get stuck, and deciding together what to fix.</p><p>If you teach a project-based course, try changing one demo prompt before rewriting the whole assignment. What could you ask a student to show you in the <em>next minute</em> that you couldn’t learn from the finished screen?</p><p><a href="https://keithwilliams.org/is117-demo-days/samples/campus-cotton/a.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Try the T-shirt storefront</a> · <a href="https://keithwilliams.org/is218-studio/projects/customer-simulator.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Meet the simulated customer</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is373-rag/blob/main/docs/students/check.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">See the two-question AI check</a></p>',17)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
